Hrm.  Keep in mind that long-term strategy tends to be what game-playing
computers are worst at in general -- grandmasters can only beat chess
computers by developing solid long-term positions.  Machine learning is
normally best at finding near-optimal solutions to small, local problems.

You can *compare* different long-term strategies by running bots many
times with each strategy and seeing which does better; but the game seems
to me to be clearly too complicated to allow a learner to figure out which
part of a losing strategy should be tweaked, and how.

